# Target A: locked-triangle fan tube bound

Date: 2026-10-03
Base main: 6c45398dcfe69e3b3bb322920a2911468dfe0573
Status: proved candidate; noncanonical. Target A and Level I remain open.

This note is disjoint from the active Target B four-bar route and the Worker-B
nine-no-root k=6 finite-support route.

It uses the exact one-ended unit-tube theorem proved and certified in commit
5a53f2b929d38a8b97f927be990d9be313f4cf57: for a unit segment e=[p,q] in a unit
triangular lattice, the lattice sites y with dist(y,e)<1 and |y-p|>=1 number at most
five.

The integer verifier for that theorem was rerun in this run and reproduced its exact
finite census: 19 sites in the radius-two lattice ball, 54 anchored six-point
candidates, and three symmetry orbits. The three orbit exclusions in that commit are
analytic.

## 1. The locked triangle costs nine sites total

Let Sigma be the Section-172 locked three-channel fan and let T_* be its central unit
equilateral triangle.

The entire open unit tube of T_* contains at most nine triangular-lattice sites. If
y,y' are two sites in that tube, choose x,x' on the triangle with |y-x|<1 and
|y'-x'|<1. Since diam(T_*)=1,

    |y-y'| < 1 + |x-x'| + 1 <= 3.

Partition the triangular lattice into the nine cosets of 3 Lambda. Distinct sites in
one coset are at distance at least three, so at most one site from each coset occurs.
Thus the three triangle edges cost at most nine sites together.

## 2. Accumulate the reaction channels from the triangle

Order every nontriangle edge by walking the reaction channels outward from the locked
triangle. Each new unit edge e=[p,q] shares p with the already exposed shield. The old
tube already contains every lattice site with |y-p|<1. Hence every new site contributed
by e is one of the at most five one-ended sites from the theorem above.

Write E=|E(Sigma)|. There are E-3 nontriangle edges, so

    |T_Sigma| <= 9 + 5(E-3) = 5E-6.

The locked fan is connected and unicyclic, hence E=|V(Sigma)|=:v. Therefore

    |T_Sigma| <= 5v-6,
    |T_Z| <= 5v-6.

This improves the current-main Section-175 deterministic bound |T_Sigma|<=9v by
4v+6 sites. It also improves the earlier general connected-shield accumulation
5E+4 by ten sites because the whole locked triangle is paid as one diameter-one core.

## 3. Right-deficiency consequence

Canonical Section 175 gives

    zeta-s >= |O_iso| + Q_Sigma - j_* - |T_Z|.

Therefore the locked full-tube branch closes whenever

    |O_iso| + Q_Sigma >= j_* + 5v - 6.

Conversely, every surviving locked-branch counterexample satisfies

    |O_iso| + Q_Sigma <= j_* + 5v - 7.

This strengthens the full-tube route, where the surviving exterior-sink sector sets
are disjoint. It does not replace the Section-177 edge-lens route, which has a smaller
lens count but a different contracted-Hall penalty and overlapping sink sectors.

## 4. Proof status and remaining bottleneck

The only finite computation used is the exact integer orbit census behind the
five-new-sites theorem. It was executed successfully in this run and returned
19 radius-two sites, 54 anchored six-sets, and three symmetry orbits. The orbit
exclusions, triangle-core bound, edge accumulation, and Section-175 propagation are
analytic. No floating-point search, CI result, heuristic, or unexecuted verifier is a
proof premise.

Target A remains open. The locked full-tube branch now needs a source/response theorem
forcing

    |O_iso| + Q_Sigma >= j_* + 5v - 6,

or a direct strict-diameter replacement.
